<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02026</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d0bc6fff-5470-462e-9965-a5a26b2752c7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Expressions in different forms can be used to define the same function. Here are three ways to define a function
  \(f\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Vertex form</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=x^2-4x+3\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-2)^2-1\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=ax^2+bx+c\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Which form would you use if you want to find the following features of the graph of \(f\)? Be prepared to explain
  your reasoning.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="9cf689e7-5f8c-4a48-ba74-2a2f83303671"><problem><para>
      <para>The \(x\)-intercepts</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Factored form</para><para>
      <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is factored form. In factored form, we can see that the \(x\)-intercepts, or
        roots, are located at \((r_1, 0)\) and \((r_2, 0)\).</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
</document>
